issues.source_body:
## Status

**PROPOSED FOUNDATIONAL RESEARCH PROGRAMME — NO POST-TRANSFORMER SUPERIORITY, LANGUAGE-COMPETENCE, PARAMETER-EFFICIENCY, QUANTUM-ADVANTAGE, NOVELTY OR PUBLICATION CLAIM IS CURRENTLY EARNED.**

Parents / coordination:
- #48 — mathematics-first conceptual development / transfer discovery;
- #49 — recursive science of scientific development;
- #50 — V2 closure, formal mechanics, recursive meta-science and paper programme;
- ORION-Q MAX: https://github.com/SzeChunYiu/ORION/issues/679;
- ORION-Q real-method programme: https://github.com/SzeChunYiu/ORION/issues/698;
- ORION-Q real-skill transfer: https://github.com/SzeChunYiu/ORION/issues/903.

This issue opens a **new foundational theory lane**. It must not disturb current V1/V2 closure work, overwrite protected outcomes, or silently absorb ORION-Q claims. It should consume existing ORION/ORION-V2/ORION-Q mechanisms only under their actual authority scopes.

---

# 0. Mission

The long-range question is deliberately ambitious:

> Can we derive, formalize and eventually instantiate a general machine-intelligence architecture in which language, reasoning, problem solving, memory, learning, verification and heterogeneous computation live natively inside ORION, while requiring materially less immutable learned capacity than a conventional Transformer for some broad, important task classes?

The immediate programme is **mathematical, not model-training-led**.

We are not starting by training a new foundation model and seeing whether benchmarks improve. We first ask what can be proved about a precisely defined ORION-native computational object, where any claimed advantage comes from an explicit mechanism rather than hidden resources.

Working name only:

`ORION Cognitive Machine (OCM)`

The name grants no novelty. If the strongest faithful parent already owns the object, rename/contract/merge rather than relabeling it.

The intended inversion is:

```text
conventional agent stack:
    foundation LLM -> wrapper/orchestrator -> tools/memory/verifier

candidate ORION machine:
    ORION = cognitive substrate
        |- learned semantic/language machinery
        |- mutable workspace/state
        |- semantic/episodic/procedural memory
        |- adaptive recurrent computation
        |- action/tool execution
        |- evidence/provenance/verification
        |- optional symbolic/formal solvers
        `- optional quantum substrate for explicitly eligible operators
```

The neural component may remain large and important. The programme does **not** assume that symbolic machinery replaces neural representation, that natural language has a tiny hand-written grammar, or that a small model can become frontier-capable without substantial learning.

---

# 1. Critical case against the idea — must remain load-bearing

A successful programme must survive the following attacks. These are not Discussion-section caveats; they are design constraints and possible terminal results.

## C1 — Turing completeness is almost scientifically useless by itself

Recurrent neural networks and suitably iterative Transformer variants can already simulate universal computation under stated assumptions. Transformer decoders with intermediate steps can become extremely expressive/Turing complete. Therefore:

`OCM_IS_TURING_COMPLETE != POST_TRANSFORMER_ADVANTAGE`.

Universality is only a calibration theorem.

## C2 — there is no blanket theorem that “Transformers cannot recurse/reason”

Transformer expressivity changes substantially with:
- encoder vs decoder;
- hard vs soft attention;
- positional encoding;
- finite vs unbounded precision;
- parameter uniformity;
- context-length assumptions;
- number of intermediate/generated steps.

Any separation theorem must name the exact comparator class. If a Transformer with the same allowed recurrent/intermediate computation simulates the OCM within comparable resources, the claimed separation contracts.

## C3 — external memory does not erase information cost

Moving facts from parameters into explicit memory may reduce **immutable core description length**, but does not remove the information. All storage, indexing, retrieval, update, bandwidth and latency must be counted.

A statement such as “2B OCM beats 70B Transformer” is invalid unless the whole organism is resource matched.

## C4 — “number of neurons” is not a rigorous capacity measure

Arbitrary real-valued weights can encode unbounded information in idealized mathematics. The programme must use finite precision / finite descriptions and count bits or another explicit representation-cost model.

## C5 — more test-time computation is not monotonically better

Recent work shows both positive test-time scaling and inverse-scaling regimes. An adaptive recurrent machine needs a stopping/routing theory and hostile cases where extra recurrence causes distraction, overfitting or state corruption.

## C6 — a semantic compiler can secretly contain the whole intelligence

If text is converted to an ideal symbolic state by an oracle-like encoder, and the OCM then solves an easy problem, the result says little about language intelligence. Compiler/renderer complexity, training assumptions and error must be explicit.

## C7 — verification can secretly solve the task

A verifier supplied with privileged labels, an exact solution, a stronger oracle, or uncosted expensive computation can make a weak generator appear strong. Verification access and cost must be included in the resource contract.

## C8 — formal-language separations may not imply useful language intelligence

A clean Dyck/parity/stack separation can be publishable theory but does not establish conversational, coding or scientific capability. Any real-language interpretation must be separately earned.

## C9 — the OCM may collapse to existing parents

Possible strongest parents include recurrent/state-space models, Universal/looped/recurrent Transformers, adaptive computation, memory-augmented neural networks, retrieval-augmented models, neural Turing machines/differentiable computers, program interpreters, production systems/global workspaces, test-time memory, modular/MoE systems and formal agent architectures.

If the OCM is only a composition or notation over these, record `PARENT_SUFFICIENT` or a narrower integration contribution.

## C10 — quantum vocabulary can create fake novelty

`SUPERPOSE`, `INTERFERE`, `MEASURE`, etc. are not quantum operations when implemented on classical distributions/branches. Reserve quantum claims for an actual quantum state/operator/resource model. State preparation, oracle construction, measurement, fault tolerance and error must be costed.

Current ORION-Q already has a stronger-oracle/resource-accounting discipline and a real-method programme. This issue should use those contracts, not create a parallel mythology.

