# 4. Resource model — required before any “smaller model” theorem

Define a whole-system resource vector, initially:

`R(O,x) = (B_theta, B_static, B_mut, T_seq, W_parallel, IO, BW, V_cost, Q_queries, E_energy)`

where:
- `B_theta`: finite bits describing immutable learned core;
- `B_static`: other immutable program/library bits;
- `B_mut`: mutable memory used for the instance/history;
- `T_seq`: sequential depth / time;
- `W_parallel`: total parallel work/FLOPs-equivalent where meaningful;
- `IO`: external retrieval/tool/oracle operations;
- `BW`: memory/inter-module communication;
- `V_cost`: verification/challenge cost;
- `Q_queries`: quantum-query/gate resource when applicable;
- `E_energy`: optional measured/estimated energy only when a defensible model exists.

Also define task-family complexity, worst-case / amortized / expected variants, and an explicit precision model.

No scalarization is mandatory. Use Pareto/non-compensatory comparison when one resource reduction hides a hard increase elsewhere.

## Full-system honesty rule

A result of the form:

`B_theta(OCM) << B_theta(Transformer)`

is not called system efficiency unless the remaining coordinates are reported. A terabyte memory and 10,000 recurrent steps may be a legitimate trade-off, but not a free win.

